<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bogd Khanate of Mongolia</h1><p class="st-meta">1911 CE – 1924 CE · capital Niislel Khuree</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Buddhist monarchy under the Bogd Khan that declared Mongolia's independence from the Qing in 1911, the forerunner of the modern Mongolian state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> broke Qing authority, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> nobles and lamas, with Russian encouragement, declared independence in December 1911 and enthroned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Eighth Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</a>, the leading lama of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, as Bogd Khan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Niislel Khüree</a> (Urga). The new state hoped to unite all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was a theocratic monarchy with ministries headed by nobles and lamas, among them the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togs-ochiryn-namnansuren">Sain Noyon Khan Namnansüren</a>. Monasteries held great wealth and a large share of the male population. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> supported it, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kyakhta-1915">Kyakhta treaty</a> of 1915 reduced it to autonomy under Chinese suzerainty. In 1919 the Chinese general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shuzheng">Xu Shuzheng</a> abolished the autonomy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1921 the White Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-von-ungern-sternberg">Baron Ungern-Sternberg</a> expelled the Chinese and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolian-peoples-party">Mongolian People's Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a> that July. The Bogd Khan reigned as a limited monarch until his death in 1924, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolian-peoples-republic">Mongolian People's Republic</a> was proclaimed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bogd-khanate-of-mongolia.jpg" alt="Bogd Khanate of Mongolia" width="320" height="392"><figcaption>Image: Joins2003 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bogd_Khaanate_Mongolia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1911 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Niislel Khuree</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bogd Khan (Eighth Jebtsundamba Khutughtu), Namnansüren (prime minister)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khalkha Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bogd Khanate, Great Mongolian State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=bogd-khanate-of-mongolia" aria-label="Bogd Khanate of Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7170 500 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bogd Khanate of Mongolia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7490 756L7567 1068M7490 756L6487 457M7490 756L8221 1549M7490 756L7490 756"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0M6487 457h0M8221 1549h0M7490 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7490 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7490 756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=bogd-khanate-of-mongolia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
